    "content": "Temporary Protected Status (TPS) is a U.S. immigration benefit granted to foreign nationals who cannot return safely to their home country due to ongoing armed conflict, environmental disasters, or extraordinary, temporary conditions. It provides lawful, temporary status, protection from deportation, and work authorization.Key Aspects of TPS:Eligibility & Requirements: Must be a national of a designated country, have filed during a specific registration period, and been continuously present in the U.S. since a specified date.Benefits: Recipients receive an Employment Authorization Document (EAD) and protection from detention or removal.Duration: Granted for 6 to 18-month periods and may be extended by the Department of Homeland Security (DHS).Limitations: TPS is temporary and does not provide a direct path to lawful permanent resident status (green card) or citizenship.Designated Countries: As of 2025-2026, countries include Afghanistan, Haiti, Venezuela, El Salvador, Honduras, Ukraine, and others.TPS is administered by the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) and was established by the Immigration Act of 1990.",
